\begin{exercise}[$\star$]\label{combinatorial:ex:simplexdist}
In $\R^{n+1}$, consider the points $e_i+e_j$, $1\le i<j\le n+1$. Show that they lie in a hyperplane and form a two-distance set of $\frac{n(n+1)}2$ points in $\R^n$. What is it for $n=3$?
\end{exercise} Exact domains and endpoint checks: Actual answer correctly notes at-most-two and small n; adopt that convention in main definition. Squared distances4-2overlap, affine hyperplane sum2 and orthonormal identification earn all n. Independent centred six vectors for n3 are opposite unit vectors on three perpendicular axes, proving regular octahedron rather than inferring shape from a drawing.

