\begin{exercise}[$\star\star$]\label{combinatorial:ex:sdistance}
For an integer $s\ge0$, an \emph{$s$-distance set} in $\R^n$ is a set whose pairwise distances take at most $s$ values. Show that it has at most $\binom{n+2s}{n}$ points.
\end{exercise} Exact domains and endpoint checks: Supplied product over actual distances gives diagonal nonzero evaluation and degree<=2s; stars-and-bars counts monomials. Independent feature-matrix rank bound agrees. r0/n0/empty/one-point/s0 and infinite-set reduction checked; final answer must explicitly extend the finite proof to infinite subsets.

