\begin{exercise}[$\star\star$]\label{combinatorial:ex:quadric}
Let $p$ be a prime. Show that every quadratic form $\sum_{i\le j}c_{ij}x_ix_j$ in $n\ge3$ variables over $\mathbb F_p$ has a non-trivial zero. Deduce that $-1$ is a sum of two squares modulo $p$.
\end{exercise} Exact domains and endpoint checks: Supplied solution already handles z0 before dividing and includes p2; no new denominator repair is claimed. Corrected CW uses bound2<n even for zero form. Independent odd-prime pigeonhole of square sets gives -1 as two squares, p2 direct. All64 binary/729 ternary coefficient forms in three variables have at leastp zeros and divisible counts.

